   3.3 Locate the existing test files that cover the affected code, and understand their format and conventions so a reproduction test would fit in naturally.

Phase 4. LOCATING THE PROBLEM: find the files and code that would need to change
   4.1 Use `grep` to search for relevant methods, classes, keywords, and error messages.
   4.2 Identify all files related to the problem statement.
   4.3 Determine the most likely location(s) where the fix belongs and explain why.

Phase 5. PLANNING: produce a concrete, actionable plan (do NOT implement it). The plan must cover:
   5.1 REPRODUCTION TESTS: how you would create a test that reproduces the issue — which test file it belongs in, what it would assert, and how it fails today.
   5.2 FIX IMPLEMENTATION: precisely what changes you would make and where (files, functions, methods), and why this approach is correct and minimal.
   5.3 TESTING THE FIX: which tests (new reproduction test plus existing related tests) you would run to verify the fix works and nothing regresses.
   5.4 RISKS AND EDGE CASES: edge cases the fix must handle and any risks or trade-offs.
   5.5 TIME ESTIMATE: estimate the total development effort needed to carry out this plan — writing the reproduction tests, implementing the fix, and verifying it. The plan above is the justification, so give a single number only. Emit this section in EXACTLY the following machine-parseable format, on its own line, so it can be extracted programmatically later. Report the duration in hours as a plain decimal number (no units, no range — a single best-estimate number):

```
### TIME_ESTIMATE
total_hours: <number>
```

Phase 6. SELF-REVIEW: critically review your own plan.
   6.1 Re-read the problem description and the <interface> section and check the plan fully addresses every requirement.
   6.2 Look for gaps, incorrect assumptions, missed files, or steps that would not actually work.
   6.3 Revise and iterate on the plan as many times as needed until you are confident it is solid, complete, and correct.

When — and only when — you are confident the plan is solid, call the `finish` tool. Pass the finalized plan as the message, organized clearly into the reproduction tests, fix implementation, testing, risks/edge-cases, and time-estimate sections described above. The message MUST include the `### TIME_ESTIMATE` block verbatim in the exact key/value format specified in Phase 5.5 so it can be parsed programmatically.

Be thorough in your exploration, reasoning, and planning. It's fine if your process is lengthy — quality and completeness of the plan are what matter. Do NOT edit the codebase or create any files.
